// `JsKind`'s home, and its only reason for being a module of its own: FFI
// Part 11 section 3 makes all ten constructors qualified-only — `JsKind.Null`,
// never a bare `Null` — and a qualified constructor is spelled through the
// module that declares it (Modules section 3.3, the `Geo.Circle(r)` mechanism).
// The union sits here so that home has the name the spec writes.

(** What JavaScript itself says a value is: `typeof` widened by the two things
    `typeof` cannot tell apart on its own — `null`, which `typeof` calls an
    object, and an array, which it also calls an object.

    This is JavaScript's classification and not Hexagon's. A `Number` may or may
    not be a whole number an `Int` can hold — that is `JsValue.toInt`'s range
    check — and there is no `Int` kind, because the `Int`/`Float` distinction is
    a Hexagon refinement `typeof` cannot see. Every constructor is reached
    qualified: `JsKind.Undefined`, `JsKind.Null`, and so on through
    `JsKind.Object`, in expressions and in patterns alike.

    `Eq` and `Show` are derived (section 3): the single-kind test
    `kind(v) == JsKind.Number` is this surface's most common question, and both
    instances are lawful and trivial on an all-nullary union. `JsValue` itself
    still has no instances — a kind is ordinary domestic data *about* a foreign
    value, not the value. *)
export union JsKind derives (Eq, Show) =
    | Undefined
    | Null
    | Bool
    | Number
    | BigInt
    | String
    | Symbol
    | Function
    | Array
    | Object
